{"affected_statement":"L1.1","objection_correct":"yes","established_elsewhere":"yes","elsewhere_quote":"Consequently the new board product is \(P'=P/g\).

- If \(g>1\), then \(P'<P\).
- If \(g=1\), the new entries are \(1\) and \(mn\). Thus \(P'=P\), while exactly two nonunits have been replaced by one, so \(K'=K-1\).","status_as_written":"false_display","repair_distance":"symbol","explanation":"The displayed equality \(\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)=mn/g^2\) is false; it should be \(mn/g\). The surrounding proof nevertheless states and uses the correct board-product update \(P'=P/g\), then handles both \(g>1\) and \(g=1\), proves well-founded descent, and separately proves exactly one nonunit survives. Replacing the exponent 2 by 1 repairs the display."}
